# ── the graphics tools a box is the only way to run ───────────────────────
#
# THE HOST HAS NO GLX. mesa here is built `-D glx=disabled -D
# platforms=wayland`, so `glxgears` and `glxinfo` — the two programs everybody
# reaches for when a machine's 3D is in question — cannot be linked on it at
# all, and neither can an X11 EGL client. Debian's mesa-utils supplies them,
# the base already carries libgl1, libegl1 and libgl1-mesa-dri, and Xwayland is
# the one X carve-out the compositor keeps: a box is the whole of what they
# need. What the row adds over the base is the demos and a handful of client
# libraries — GLES, X11, xkbcommon, libdecor — and no driver blob.
#
# IT IS ALSO THE HOST-VERSUS-BOX MEASUREMENT. The pack's X11 flavours
# (es2gears_x11, eglgears_x11, xeglgears) run the same test as the host's
# native es2gears_wayland through Xwayland and a container, so the difference
# between the two numbers is what that path costs — and it is a number nothing
# else on the machine produces.
app app.mesa-utils   base   mesa-utils
# mesa-utils ships no desktop entry, and these are the three names the host
# does NOT carry, which is what makes them safe to put on the PATH: a shim
# named for a binary the host has too would shadow it, and somebody
# benchmarking would silently measure the box. The pack's `eglinfo`,
# `es2gears_wayland` and `vkgears` collide with the mesa-demos port and are
# deliberately left without a row — `kdos-appbox -b app.mesa-utils run
# <binary>` reaches those when the boxed copy is the one wanted.
cmd app.mesa-utils glxgears
cmd app.mesa-utils glxinfo
cmd app.mesa-utils es2gears_x11
#
# glmark2 AND vulkan-tools ARE NOT ROWS, and that is the same rule applied
# twice. Both are native ports in script/04_phase4/packages.txt, so the host
# already has glmark2-wayland, glmark2-es2-wayland, vkcube and vulkaninfo
# under those exact names; trixie/amd64 carries no GLX flavour of glmark2, so
# a boxed copy would be the same two Wayland binaries. A boxed vulkan-tools
# additionally needs mesa-vulkan-drivers — 241 MB and 26 packages — because a
# box sees none of the host's ICDs, and it would buy the same two commands the
# host installs.

# ── groups ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A group is a name somebody picks software by, and it is the unit both the
# store and kinstall offer. The FIRST row for a group id is its description;
# every later row lists members, and a member may be in more than one group.
#
# Every member must be an `app` or `data` row in this file. One that is not is
# an install the resolver refuses by name — a group is not a place to record
# software that is not carried.
#
# `essential` is what a default install suggests and what kinstall preselects.
# The bases and the runtimes are in NO group: they arrive through the parent
# chain of whatever is chosen, and an application whose runtime was left out is
# an application that cannot start.
